#!/bin/sh
# Run this project's build through whatever wrapper the machine provides, or plainly if it provides
# none.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. On 2026-09-16 nine agent sessions worked this repository from eight worktrees.
# Each worktree is its own compilation, so the machine was running eight sbt JVMs at once: two
# coverage runs were killed, and a run that takes two minutes took twelve. That much was known and a
# machine-wide cap on concurrent builds was written to answer it. What was not answered is that the
# cap lives outside every checkout and the instructions in `AGENTS.md` did not name it, so the
# sixteen commands a session copies from the project's own documentation started builds that never
# asked the cap for anything. Measured that evening: five of the seven sbt JVMs running were outside
# it, while the cap itself read two slots of two held all night. **The cap was not exceeded, it was
# bypassed**, and by sessions doing exactly what they were told to do. That distinction is what
# decides the remedy: a sentence asking people to remember would be another agreement, and this file
# exists so that copying the command is enough.
#
# WHY AN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE AND NOT THE WRAPPER'S PATH. The wrapper on this machine belongs to a
# practice that is not part of this repository and does not travel with it. A tracked file naming
# its path would put a private layout into something that can be handed to somebody else, and would
# also be wrong on the first machine that arranges things differently. So the repository knows only
# that a wrapper may exist and where to ask: the machine names it, and a machine that names nothing
# gets a plain `sbt`, told so on stderr and otherwise losing nothing.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOT. It is not a cap of its own. It holds no lock, counts nothing, and cannot make two
# builds wait for each other; everything it does is hand the command to whatever was named and get
# out of the way. If `SYMBOLUM_BUILD_WRAPPER` is unset this is `sbt` with one extra process and one
# line of stderr, which is the case a clone with no such practice around it lands in, and that case
# is supposed to work.
#
# WHY IT SAYS SO WHEN IT IS NOT QUEUEING, which was the other design and was argued out of it by a
# second session writing the same file the same night. Falling through silently gives a caller the
# shape of being capped without the fact of it, and believing you are in a queue you are not in is
# the exact confusion this whole change exists to end: five of seven builds on 2026-09-16 were
# outside the cap and every one of their sessions thought it was doing the right thing. So an
# unqueued run is announced on stderr, every time, and the cost of that is one line on a machine
# that has no cap to ask.
#
# The argument that settled it is one this repository already makes about itself twice, and it is
# stronger than the convenience it costs. `Refutation` keeps seven answers apart and
# `LogicError.Standing` keeps three, and both say in writing that collapsing two of them turns "I
# could not ask" into "I asked and it was fine". A silent fall-through is that collapse, performed
# by a file called `tools/sbt`: a reader who takes the name to mean the cap is not being careless,
# they are reading it the way it is written. `tools/sbt-test.sh` asserts the line rather than the
# silence, so the decision is checked and not merely recorded here.
#
# THE INTERFACE IT EXPECTS, stated because a wrapper that does not offer it will look broken here:
# `"$SYMBOLUM_BUILD_WRAPPER" -- sbt <args>` runs `sbt <args>` under whatever discipline it applies,
# passes its arguments through untouched, and exits with the build's own status. `--` separates the
# wrapper's options from the command, so a wrapper is free to take options of its own.
#
# Usage:
#   tools/sbt test
#   tools/sbt "tactics/runMain garden.xeno.symbolum.tactics.Ask !x:real. x + 0 = x"

# WHY IT ALSO LOOKS AT WHAT ELSE IS RUNNING, added 2026-09-18 because the announcement below was
# read sixteen times by somebody who then concluded the opposite of it. The line says *this build*
# is uncapped and it gets read as *this machine* has no cap, and the two are the distinction this
# repository keeps seven answers apart for one floor down: "I could not ask" is not "I asked and it
# was fine". The session that wrote this card spent seven hours outside a queue that was running the
# whole time, went and checked, and checked with an instrument that could not read — `find` over a
# symlinked directory, which answers nothing and looks like an absence.
#
# So the fall-through now says WHICH of the two it is, and it finds out without this file knowing
# anything about the machine's layout. A build that HAS a wrapper leaves the path in a file under
# `$TMPDIR`, and a build that has none reads it. What is written down here is the rendezvous — a
# name this repository chooses — and never the wrapper's own path, which arrives from whichever
# session on this machine was configured.
#
# WHY `$TMPDIR` AND NOT `/tmp`, measured on 2026-09-18: `$TMPDIR` on macOS is `drwx------` and the
# user's own, where `/tmp` is world-writable with the sticky bit. The fallback for a machine that
# names no `$TMPDIR` is `/tmp` with the user id in the file name, where another user can get there
# first — which is survivable only because of the rule in the paragraph below, that what is found is
# printed and never run.
#
# The environment of the sbt processes already running is read as well, and second, because it is
# the one thing a file cannot say: that a build is capped **now** rather than that one was once. A
# machine where the file is missing and something capped is running is a machine whose builds
# predate this paragraph.
#
# IT REPORTS AND NEVER ACTS ON WHAT IT FINDS. Taking a program to exec out of another process's
# environment would be this file deciding on its own to run something nobody in this session named.
# The difference between telling a caller what to do and doing it for them is the whole of why this
# file exists rather than a line in `AGENTS.md`.

set -eu

# Where a capped build leaves the path and an uncapped one looks for it.
#
# The user id is in the name for the `/tmp` case, so that two people on one machine do not write
# each other's rendezvous; under `$TMPDIR` it is redundant and harmless.
rendezvous="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/symbolum-build-wrapper.$(id -u 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)"

# What a capped build recorded, when it is still there and still runs.
#
# The `-x` is not tidiness: a path recorded weeks ago and since moved would otherwise be printed as
# the thing to do, which is the failure this whole change is about arriving from the other side.
recorded() {
    [ -r "$rendezvous" ] || return 1
    remembered=$(head -1 "$rendezvous" 2>/dev/null) || return 1
    [ -n "$remembered" ] || return 1
    [ -x "$remembered" ] || return 1
    printf '%s\n' "$remembered"
}

# The cap another build on this machine was given, printed, when there is one to see.
elsewhere() {
    running=$(pgrep -f sbt-launch 2>/dev/null) || return 1

    for process in $running; do
        named=$(ps eww -o command= -p "$process" 2>/dev/null |
            tr ' ' '\n' |
            sed -n 's/^SYMBOLUM_BUILD_WRAPPER=//p' |
            sed -n '1p') || named=""
        if [ -n "$named" ]; then
            printf '%s\n' "$named"
            return 0
        fi
    done

    return 1
}

if [ -n "${SYMBOLUM_BUILD_WRAPPER:-}" ]; then
    if [ -x "$SYMBOLUM_BUILD_WRAPPER" ]; then
        # Left for the next session on this machine, best effort and never fatal: a build that
        # cannot write a temporary file is still a build, and the thing being recorded is a
        # convenience for somebody else rather than anything this run depends on.
        printf '%s\n' "$SYMBOLUM_BUILD_WRAPPER" > "$rendezvous" 2>/dev/null || true
        exec "$SYMBOLUM_BUILD_WRAPPER" -- sbt "$@"
    fi
    echo "tools/sbt: SYMBOLUM_BUILD_WRAPPER names '$SYMBOLUM_BUILD_WRAPPER', which is not" >&2
    echo "tools/sbt: executable, so nothing is capping this build." >&2
else
    echo "tools/sbt: SYMBOLUM_BUILD_WRAPPER is unset, so nothing is capping this build." >&2
fi

# Three answers and not two, for the reason the paragraph above gives: a machine with a queue this
# session is not in, a machine where nothing is queueing, and a machine this session cannot see well
# enough to say either. Collapsing the last into the middle is what the sentence above already does.
if seen=$(recorded || elsewhere); then
    echo "tools/sbt: a build on this machine IS capped, by '$seen', so there is a queue and this" >&2
    echo "tools/sbt: session is not in it: prefix the command with SYMBOLUM_BUILD_WRAPPER=$seen" >&2
elif pgrep -f sbt-launch >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "tools/sbt: no other build running here names a cap either, so nothing on this machine" >&2
    echo "tools/sbt: is queueing at the moment." >&2
else
    echo "tools/sbt: and no other build is running here, nor has one left a cap behind, so whether" >&2
    echo "tools/sbt: this machine has a queue at all is not something this session can see." >&2
fi

exec sbt "$@"
